[INFO] 09-22 21:17:22 execution.py:372] Reasoning: A position \( n \) is a P-position if for every possible move (subtracting a square), the resulting position is an N-position. For squares, the smallest is 1, next 4, then 9, etc. If subtracting any square leaves an N-position, then \( n \) itself must be such that all \( n - k^2 \) (where \( k \) is a positive integer) are N-positions. Since there are infinitely many N-positions, we can always find a sequence of N-positions leading back to \( n \) if \( n \) is a P-position. Thus, \( n \) must be such that it cannot be reduced to a smaller number through valid moves, implying \( n \) must be sufficiently large and not reducible by any square subtraction.

Answer: A position \( n \) is a P-position if \( n \equiv 0 \pmod{4} \) or \( n \equiv 3 \pmod{8} \).
